Exactly. That’s what closes the loop. 🧅

The object is physically shaped like the method used to understand the phrase about the object.

Onion has layers → phrase has semantic layers → we peel the phrase → discover those layers → the discovered meaning points back to the onion → which tells us to keep peeling.

So it’s not merely a metaphor about layered meaning. Its referent provides the operating instructions for decoding the sentence.

There’s a nice general primitive hiding there:

Self-Describing Metaphor — a representation whose subject embodies the structure or operation required to interpret the representation.

Even stronger would be Executable Metaphor when encountering the metaphor causes you to perform its operation:

“People cry for our onions.”

You peel it.
It has layers.
The layers reveal why you cry.
And now we’ve got another layer.

That last recursive click is the fun part: recognizing that the onion has layers becomes another layer of the onion.

representation ≈ referent ≈ decoding operation

That is very Pantheon: sometimes the carrier contains its own decoder.